---
// Static route: takes precedence over the [slug] method template, because the
// method collection has no "choosing-a-method" record. The URL stays under
// /methods/ so the decision guide reads as the front door to the method pages.
import Layout from '~/layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import Sources from '~/components/common/Sources.astro';
import StructuredData from '~/components/common/StructuredData.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '~/components/common/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import ReviewStatement from '~/components/common/ReviewStatement.astro';
import { SITE } from 'astrowind:config';
import { authorRef, dateFields, organizationRef, siteUrl } from '~/utils/schema';
import { seoDescription, seoTitle } from '~/utils/seo';
import CallToAction from '~/components/widgets/CallToAction.astro';

const site = siteUrl(SITE.site);
const reviewed = '19 November 2026';

const metadata = {
  title: seoTitle('How to Choose a Quit Smoking Method'),
  description: seoDescription(
    'A practical framework for choosing a quit method: cold turkey, NRT, prescription medication and behavioural support, compared by access, cost and fit.'
  ),
};

const pageSchema = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Article',
    headline: 'How to Choose a Quit Smoking Method',
    name: 'How to Choose a Quit Smoking Method',
    description:
      'A decision guide to smoking cessation methods: what the guidelines recommend, how the evidence compares, and how to match a method to your access, history and preferences.',
    url: `${site}/methods/choosing-a-method/`,
    mainEntityOfPage: `${site}/methods/choosing-a-method/`,
    isPartOf: { '@id': `${site}/#website` },
    author: authorRef(SITE.site),
    publisher: organizationRef(SITE.site),
    ...dateFields(undefined, '2026-11-19'),
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
    headline: 'How to Choose a Quit Smoking Method',
    name: 'How to Choose a Quit Smoking Method',
    description:
      'How to compare cessation methods: guideline recommendations, evidence strength, access, side effects and personal history. General education, not a prescription.',
    url: `${site}/methods/choosing-a-method/`,
    mainEntityOfPage: `${site}/methods/choosing-a-method/`,
    isPartOf: { '@id': `${site}/#website` },
    author: authorRef(SITE.site),
    publisher: organizationRef(SITE.site),
    about: { '@type': 'MedicalCondition', name: 'Tobacco use disorder' },
    audience: { '@type': 'Audience', audienceType: 'Adults who smoke and want to stop' },
    ...dateFields(undefined, '2026-11-19'),
  },
];

const pageSources = [
  {
    label: 'WHO — Clinical treatment guideline for tobacco cessation in adults (2024)',
    url: 'https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240096431',
  },
  {
    label: 'USPSTF — Tobacco smoking cessation in adults, including pregnant persons (2021)',
    url: 'https://www.uspreventiveservicestaskforce.org/uspstf/recommendation/tobacco-use-in-adults-and-pregnant-women-counseling-and-interventions',
  },
  {
    label: 'NICE NG209 — Tobacco: preventing uptake, promoting quitting and treating dependence',
    url: 'https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng209',
  },
  {
    label: 'Cochrane — Nicotine replacement therapy for smoking cessation (R-001)',
    url: 'https://www.cochranelibrary.com/cdsr/doi/10.1002/14651858.CD000146.pub4/full',
  },
  {
    label: 'Cochrane — Different doses, durations and modes of delivery of nicotine replacement therapy (2023)',
    url: 'https://www.cochranelibrary.com/cdsr/doi/10.1002/14651858.CD013308.pub2/full',
  },
  {
    label: 'Cochrane — Nicotine receptor partial agonists for smoking cessation (R-002)',
    url: 'https://www.cochranelibrary.com/cdsr/doi/10.1002/14651858.CD006103.pub4/full',
  },
  {
    label: 'Cochrane — Reduction versus abrupt cessation in smokers who want to quit (R-004)',
    url: 'https://www.cochranelibrary.com/cdsr/doi/10.1002/14651858.CD010332.pub2/full',
  },
  {
    label: 'US Surgeon General — Smoking Cessation: A Report of the Surgeon General (2020)',
    url: 'https://www.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/2020-cessation-sgr-full-report.pdf',
  },
  {
    label: 'NHS — Quit smoking',
    url: 'https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/quit-smoking/',
  },
];

const tableHead = 'py-2 pr-4 font-bold text-heading dark:text-slate-200';
const tableRow = 'border-b border-gray-100 dark:border-slate-800';
const tableCell = 'py-3 pr-4 align-top';
const inline = 'text-primary underline decoration-1 underline-offset-2';
---

<Layout metadata={metadata}>
  <StructuredData slot="head" schema={pageSchema} />
  <Breadcrumbs
    bar
    items={[
      { text: 'Home', href: '/' },
      { text: 'Quit Methods', href: '/methods/' },
      { text: 'How to choose a method' },
    ]}
  />

  <section class="relative mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 sm:px-6 pt-8 md:pt-12 pb-4">
    <p class="text-base font-bold tracking-wide uppercase text-secondary mb-2 text-center">Decision Guide</p>
    <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold leading-tighter tracking-tighter font-heading text-heading text-center">
      How to choose a quit smoking method
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl mx-auto text-lg text-muted text-center">
      The strongest method on paper is worthless if you cannot get it, cannot tolerate it, or will not keep using it.
      This guide narrows the field in the order that actually matters.
    </p>
  </section>
  <p class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 sm:px-6 pb-2 text-xs text-muted dark:text-slate-400">
    Maintained by <span class="font-semibold">SmokingCessationHub Tech Team</span> · Last updated
    <span class="font-semibold">{reviewed}</span> · Not reviewed by a clinician
  </p>

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 sm:px-6 py-8">
    <div class="rounded-lg border border-gray-200 dark:border-slate-700 bg-gray-50 dark:bg-slate-900/40 p-5 text-sm mb-10">
      <p class="text-muted dark:text-slate-400">
        <span class="font-bold text-heading dark:text-slate-200">What this page is:</span> a summary of public guidance
        and systematic reviews on choosing a cessation method, written for adults who smoke and want to stop. It is
        general education, not a personal treatment plan. It contains no doses, no tapering schedules and no injection
        instructions, and it does not guarantee that any method will work for you. Medication decisions belong with a
        clinician or pharmacist who knows your history. Read our
        <a href="/editorial-policy/" class={inline}>editorial policy</a> for how sources are selected and corrected.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="space-y-12">
      <div>
        <p class="text-muted dark:text-slate-400">
          People usually ask which method works best. The honest answer is that the question is incomplete. A method
          has to be available, affordable, tolerable and used for long enough to matter. Varenicline has the strongest
          average effect against a single form of nicotine replacement, but it needs a prescriber and a course you can
          stay on. Combination NRT performs well in trials, but only if you use two forms consistently rather than
          rationing one. Cold turkey is free and immediate, and it is also the method most often attempted without any
          preparation at all.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-muted dark:text-slate-400">
          This page gives you a way to choose in the right order: first what the guidance says, then what the evidence
          can and cannot distinguish, then the practical questions — access, health history, past attempts, and how
          much structure you actually want. The method pages and the head-to-head
          <a href="/comparisons/" class={inline}>comparisons</a> hold the detail for each option. This page is the
          decision layer above them.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold font-heading mb-4">1. Start with what the guidelines agree on</h2>
        <p class="text-muted dark:text-slate-400">
          The major guidelines differ in emphasis, but they converge on one structural point: the best-supported
          approach is <strong class="text-heading">behavioural support combined with medication</strong>, not either
          one alone.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-muted dark:text-slate-400">
          The WHO's 2024 clinical treatment guideline for tobacco cessation recommends behavioural support alongside
          pharmacotherapy, and lists nicotine replacement therapy, varenicline, bupropion and cytisine among the
          treatment options. The USPSTF's 2021 recommendation asks clinicians to advise all adults who smoke to stop
          and to offer behavioural interventions and approved pharmacotherapy; for pregnant people it recommends
          behavioural interventions and finds the evidence on medication insufficient, which makes medication a
          clinician's decision rather than a default. NICE NG209 likewise recommends combining behavioural support
          with medication, and it is the guidance that most clearly allows e-cigarettes as a stop-smoking option.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-muted dark:text-slate-400">
          Two practical conclusions follow. If you are choosing between a medication and a behavioural programme, the
          answer is usually neither-or: it is both, if both are accessible. And if you are choosing cold turkey because
          you do not want to involve a clinician, that is a legitimate preference — but it is a choice about access,
          not evidence that support is unnecessary.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-muted dark:text-slate-400">
          The <a href="/quit-smoking/" class={inline}>complete guide to quitting</a> covers the sequence — quit date,
          preparation, the first days, the first year — rather than the menu. Use it after you have picked a route,
          or read it first if you have not decided whether you are ready.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold font-heading mb-4">2. What the evidence can actually distinguish</h2>
        <p class="text-muted dark:text-slate-400">
          Evidence rankings are useful only at the resolution they can support. The reliable findings are broad;
          the fine distinctions usually are not.
        </p>
        <ul class="mt-4 list-disc list-inside space-y-3 text-muted dark:text-slate-400">
          <li>
            <strong class="text-heading">Any nicotine replacement beats none.</strong> The Cochrane review of NRT
            finds that every form — patch, gum, lozenge, inhalator, spray — increases quit rates compared with placebo
            or no NRT.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-heading">Combination NRT beats a single form.</strong> Using a patch plus a
            fast-acting form is more effective than one form alone (risk ratio about 1.27 in the 2023 Cochrane review
            of doses, durations and delivery modes; high certainty). That is a reasonably strong, practical finding.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-heading">Varenicline beats single-form NRT, and is roughly equal to combination
            NRT.</strong> The 2023 partial-agonist review found a clear advantage over a single NRT form and no clear
            difference against dual-form NRT, where the certainty was low. Do not compress that into a general
            ranking.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-heading">Behavioural support adds to medication.</strong> Structured support —
            quitline calls, group programmes, text programmes, face-to-face services — improves outcomes, and the
            guidelines recommend combining it with medication rather than substituting one for the other.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-heading">Head-to-head rankings below the top tier are uncertain.</strong> Cytisine
            versus varenicline, bupropion versus NRT, and most network rankings rest on indirect or limited evidence.
            A method that is "second best" on average may still be the best one available to you.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mt-4 text-muted dark:text-slate-400">
          What the evidence cannot tell you is which method you will tolerate, which one fits your working day, and
          whether you will still be using it in week four. Those are the variables that decide most outcomes.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold font-heading mb-4">3. Five questions that narrow the field</h2>
        <p class="text-muted dark:text-slate-400 mb-4">
          Answer these in order. Each one removes options that were never realistic, which is more useful than
          ranking the ones that remain.
        </p>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="w-full text-sm text-left">
            <thead>
              <tr class="border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-slate-700">
                <th class={tableHead}>Question</th>
                <th class={tableHead}>Why it matters</th>
                <th class={tableHead}>What it usually points to</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="text-muted dark:text-slate-400">
              <tr class={tableRow}>
                <td class={`${tableCell} font-semibold text-heading dark:text-slate-300`}>
                  Can you see a prescriber in the next two weeks?
                </td>
                <td class={tableCell}>
                  Prescription medicines need a consultation, and some need monitoring or a follow-up. If access is
                  slow, that changes the timeline, not the value of the option.
                </td>
                <td class={tableCell}>
                  Yes: varenicline, bupropion and cytisine become real options. No: NRT, behavioural support and
                  quitlines carry the plan.
                </td>
              </tr>
              <tr class={tableRow}>
                <td class={`${tableCell} font-semibold text-heading dark:text-slate-300`}>
                  Have you tried a method before, and what happened?
                </td>
                <td class={tableCell}>
                  A previous attempt is information about fit and dose of support, not proof that you cannot quit.
                  Repeating the same under-supported attempt usually repeats the same result.
                </td>
                <td class={tableCell}>
                  Failed cold turkey: add support or medication. Failed single NRT: consider combination NRT. Stopped
                  varenicline early: discuss tolerability before choosing it again.
                </td>
              </tr>
              <tr class={tableRow}>
                <td class={`${tableCell} font-semibold text-heading dark:text-slate-300`}>
                  Do you have a health condition or take medicines that need checking?
                </td>
                <td class={tableCell}>
                  Pregnancy, cardiovascular disease, mental-health conditions and some drug interactions change which
                  options are appropriate.
                </td>
                <td class={tableCell}>
                  Ask a clinician or pharmacist before starting any medicine. See the
                  <a href="/populations/" class={inline}>special situations</a> pages for the general principles.
                </td>
              </tr>
              <tr class={tableRow}>
                <td class={`${tableCell} font-semibold text-heading dark:text-slate-300`}>
                  How much structure do you want?
                </td>
                <td class={tableCell}>
                  Some people want a course to follow and appointments to keep. Others need something they can start
                  alone, today. Both are valid; they lead to different plans.
                </td>
                <td class={tableCell}>
                  High structure: varenicline or bupropion plus a programme. Low structure: NRT plus a quitline or text
                  support. Minimal: a written cold-turkey plan with a support contact.
                </td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class={`${tableCell} font-semibold text-heading dark:text-slate-300`}>
                  What can you afford for the length of the course?
                </td>
                <td class={tableCell}>
                  Cost decides adherence more often than preference does. A cheaper option used properly beats an
                  expensive one abandoned in week two.
                </td>
                <td class={tableCell}>
                  Check local subsidy or insurance. Generic NRT is often the lowest-cost supported option; quitlines are
                  usually free.
                </td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold font-heading mb-4">4. Matching the method to the situation</h2>
        <p class="text-muted dark:text-slate-400 mb-4">
          The table is a starting point for a conversation, not a prescription. Several rows can apply at once.
        </p>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="w-full text-sm text-left">
            <thead>
              <tr class="border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-slate-700">
                <th class={tableHead}>If this describes you</th>
                <th class={tableHead}>Reasonable first options</th>
                <th class={tableHead}>Why</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="text-muted dark:text-slate-400">
              <tr class={tableRow}>
                <td class={`${tableCell} font-semibold text-heading dark:text-slate-300`}>
                  First serious attempt, no strong preference
                </td>
                <td class={tableCell}>
                  Behavioural support plus NRT; or cold turkey with a written plan and a quitline contact
                </td>
                <td class={tableCell}>
                  Both routes are low-barrier. The support component is what most first attempts lack, not motivation.
                </td>
              </tr>
              <tr class={tableRow}>
                <td class={`${tableCell} font-semibold text-heading dark:text-slate-300`}>
                  Previous attempt ended in the first week
                </td>
                <td class={tableCell}>
                  Combination NRT, or a prescriber discussion about varenicline or bupropion
                </td>
                <td class={tableCell}>
                  Early relapse usually means the withdrawal load was higher than the plan allowed for. Strengthen the
                  pharmacological side before trying harder.
                </td>
              </tr>
              <tr class={tableRow}>
                <td class={`${tableCell} font-semibold text-heading dark:text-slate-300`}>
                  Heavy smoking, strong morning craving
                </td>
                <td class={tableCell}>
                  Combination NRT with a fast-acting form; ask about prescription options
                </td>
                <td class={tableCell}>
                  A steady patch plus a form you can use at the peak is the standard way to cover both background and
                  breakthrough craving.
                </td>
              </tr>
              <tr class={tableRow}>
                <td class={`${tableCell} font-semibold text-heading dark:text-slate-300`}>
                  Dislikes medication or wants to avoid it
                </td>
                <td class={tableCell}>
                  Structured behavioural support, quitline, text programme, cold turkey
                </td>
                <td class={tableCell}>
                  Support alone is better than an unsupported attempt. Say plainly that the evidence for medication is
                  stronger, then plan the support properly.
                </td>
              </tr>
              <tr class={tableRow}>
                <td class={`${tableCell} font-semibold text-heading dark:text-slate-300`}>
                  Pregnant or planning pregnancy
                </td>
                <td class={tableCell}>
                  Behavioural support first; medication only through a clinician
                </td>
                <td class={tableCell}>
                  USPSTF finds behavioural interventions recommended and pharmacotherapy evidence insufficient. That
                  is a risk-benefit conversation, not a self-service choice.
                </td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class={`${tableCell} font-semibold text-heading dark:text-slate-300`}>
                  Serious mental-health condition
                </td>
                <td class={tableCell}>
                  Behavioural support plus a clinician-guided medication decision
                </td>
                <td class={tableCell}>
                  Stopping smoking is still worth doing; the medicine choice and monitoring need to account for
                  existing treatment. See
                  <a href="/populations/mental-health-conditions/" class={inline}>mental health and quitting</a>.
                </td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold font-heading mb-4">5. What behavioural support means in practice</h2>
        <p class="text-muted dark:text-slate-400">
          "Behavioural support" is a vague phrase for a set of concrete services. It usually means a trained adviser
          who helps you set a quit date, prepares you for specific triggers, reviews what happened after a lapse, and
          keeps contact for a few weeks. The delivery channel matters less than whether it is structured and repeated.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-muted dark:text-slate-400">
          The main routes are:
        </p>
        <ul class="mt-4 list-disc list-inside space-y-2 text-muted dark:text-slate-400">
          <li>
            <strong class="text-heading">Telephone quitlines</strong> — free in many countries, often with callback
            sessions. See <a href="/methods/quitlines/" class={inline}>quitlines</a>.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-heading">Face-to-face or group programmes</strong> — stop-smoking services, pharmacy
            programmes and community groups. See <a href="/methods/counseling/" class={inline}>counselling</a>.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-heading">Text and app programmes</strong> — structured messages timed around the quit
            date. Convenient, but the evidence is more variable than for telephone support. See
            <a href="/methods/digital-programs/" class={inline}>digital programmes</a>.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-heading">Peer support</strong> — group or online communities. Useful for persistence;
            not a substitute for clinical advice. See <a href="/methods/peer-support/" class={inline}>peer support</a>.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mt-4 text-muted dark:text-slate-400">
          If you only do one thing beyond choosing a method, book the support before the quit date. Support arranged
          after the first bad day is support you may not use.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold font-heading mb-4">6. Medication: what to ask a clinician or pharmacist</h2>
        <p class="text-muted dark:text-slate-400">
          This page does not give doses or schedules. What it can do is tell you which questions make a consultation
          useful, so you leave with a plan rather than a leaflet.
        </p>
        <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal list-inside space-y-2 text-muted dark:text-slate-400">
          <li>Which options are appropriate given my health history, other medicines and pregnancy status?</li>
          <li>What is the intended length of the course, and what happens if I miss a day or want to stop early?</li>
          <li>Which side effects should make me call rather than wait — and which are common and settle?</li>
          <li>Can I combine this with NRT, and if so, which form and when?</li>
          <li>What support is available alongside it, and how do I access that here?</li>
          <li>If this does not work, what is the next option rather than the same option again?</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="mt-4 text-muted dark:text-slate-400">
          The <a href="/medications/" class={inline}>medications section</a> compares the classes at the level the
          evidence supports: how they work, what the trials found, common adverse effects and cautions. It is
          deliberately not a prescribing reference.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold font-heading mb-4">7. Cold turkey and gradual reduction: when either is reasonable</h2>
        <p class="text-muted dark:text-slate-400">
          Cold turkey is not a failure of planning by definition. It is a reasonable choice when you have a clear quit
          date, a prepared environment, at least one person who knows what you are doing, and a plan for the first
          three days. What makes it fail is usually the absence of those things, not the absence of a product.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-muted dark:text-slate-400">
          Gradual reduction is reasonable when a fixed quit date feels impossible, or when you want to separate the
          behavioural work from the withdrawal peak. The Cochrane review of reduction versus abrupt cessation found
          that abrupt cessation is at least as effective and may be better for people who are able to set a date — but
          reduction with support is a legitimate route for people who are not there yet. The
          <a href="/comparisons/cold-turkey-vs-gradual-reduction/" class={inline}>full comparison</a> sets out the
          trade-offs, and the individual method pages cover
          <a href="/methods/cold-turkey/" class={inline}>cold turkey</a> and
          <a href="/methods/gradual-reduction/" class={inline}>gradual reduction</a>.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-muted dark:text-slate-400">
          One caution applies to both: if you cut down while still smoking, you can end up maintaining the habit at a
          lower rate indefinitely. Set a date for zero, not a direction of travel.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold font-heading mb-4">8. Combining methods without overcomplicating</h2>
        <p class="text-muted dark:text-slate-400">
          Most successful plans combine two things: something pharmacological to reduce withdrawal, and something
          behavioural to change the situations that trigger smoking. A third element — social accountability — is
          optional but cheap.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-muted dark:text-slate-400">
          A workable combination looks like this: a medication or NRT chosen with advice; a support contact booked
          before the quit date; a written plan for the first 72 hours; and a review point at the end of week one. That
          is not a complex programme. It is the minimum structure the guidelines describe, translated into four
          calendar entries.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-muted dark:text-slate-400">
          The combination to avoid is "NRT in a drawer and no plan". Product without technique is the most common
          expensive mistake in cessation: the patch is used sporadically, the gum is chewed like normal gum, the
          lozenge is bitten, and the conclusion drawn is that NRT does not work.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold font-heading mb-4">9. A decision worksheet you can finish in ten minutes</h2>
        <p class="text-muted dark:text-slate-400 mb-4">
          Write the answers down. The act of writing produces a plan that a preference in your head does not.
        </p>
        <ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-3 text-muted dark:text-slate-400">
          <li>
            <strong class="text-heading">Set the date.</strong> Pick a specific day in the next two to three weeks. A
            date that is too soon has no preparation; a date that keeps moving is not a date. The
            <a href="/questions/how-do-i-choose-a-quit-date/" class={inline}>quit-date guide</a> has a checklist.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-heading">Choose the support before the product.</strong> Name the service, the phone
            number or the person. "I will look into it" is not support.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-heading">Choose the medication route with advice, or consciously choose none.</strong>{' '}
            If none, say why, and what you will do instead when withdrawal peaks.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-heading">Write three if-then rules.</strong> "If I am in the car after work, then I
            will..." Specific situations beat general intentions.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-heading">Set the review.</strong> A ten-minute check at day three and day seven, with
            yourself or your support contact. Decide in advance what you will change if it is going badly.
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p class="mt-4 text-muted dark:text-slate-400">
          The <a href="/start/" class={inline}>start-here path</a> walks through the same steps with links to the
          preparation pages, and the <a href="/withdrawal/timeline/" class={inline}>withdrawal timeline</a> tells you
          what the first days are likely to feel like so the plan is not built on optimism.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold font-heading mb-4">10. Common selection mistakes</h2>
        <ul class="mt-2 list-disc list-inside space-y-3 text-muted dark:text-slate-400">
          <li>
            <strong class="text-heading">Choosing the method with the best average result rather than the best fit.</strong>{' '}
            A treatment you abandon in week two has an average result of zero for you.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-heading">Treating a previous failure as a verdict.</strong> Most people who quit
            needed more than one attempt. The useful question is what the attempt lacked, not what it proves.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-heading">Starting medication on the quit date without reading how to use it.</strong>{' '}
            Technique matters, especially for oral NRT. Read the label and ask the pharmacist.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-heading">Planning the quit but not the relapse.</strong> Decide now what you will do
            after a cigarette — not whether you will have one. The
            <a href="/relapse/" class={inline}>relapse section</a> covers the difference between a lapse and a
            return to smoking.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-heading">Waiting for the perfect week.</strong> There is no week without a deadline,
            a social event or a bad night's sleep. Preparation can be scheduled; life cannot.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold font-heading mb-4">11. If the first choice does not work</h2>
        <p class="text-muted dark:text-slate-400">
          A failed attempt is not a reason to choose a harder method next time. It is a reason to change one variable:
          add behavioural support if you had none, switch from a single NRT form to combination NRT, or discuss a
          prescription option if you have only used over-the-counter products. Changing everything at once makes it
          impossible to know what helped.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-muted dark:text-slate-400">
          If you stopped a medicine because of side effects, say so at the next consultation. "It made me feel awful"
          is clinically useful information; it may lead to a different medicine, a different schedule or a different
          class entirely. Do not silently switch to a method you already know does not suit you.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold font-heading mb-4">12. Bottom line</h2>
        <p class="text-muted dark:text-slate-400">
          Choose in this order: the support you can actually access, the medication or NRT you can tolerate and afford,
          the quit date you can prepare for, and the plan for the first 72 hours. The evidence favours behavioural
          support plus medication; it does not favour waiting until you can arrange the perfect version of either.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-muted dark:text-slate-400">
          If you remember one sentence from this page, make it this: the method is not the quit. The method is what
          carries you through the first three days and the first three weeks while the new routine takes hold.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

